'''baronage''' (bär′ọn-āj), ''n.'' [< ME. ''baronage'', ''barunage'', ''barnage'', < OF. ''barnage'', ''barnaige'', F. ''baronnage'' = Pr. ''barnatge'' = It. ''baronaggio'', ''barnaggio'' (ML. reflex ''baronagium''), < ML. *''baronaticum'', < ''baro(n-)'': see ''baron'' and ''-age''.] '''1.''' The whole body of British barons; formerly, the nobility or peerage in general.
{{smaller block|The ''baronage'' is divided so narrowly that the summons or exclusion of half a dozen members changes the fate of a ministry or of a dynasty.
{{right|''Stubbs'', Const. Hist., § 686.}}}}
